{"kind":"assert","text":"R14 pin-capability answer at Main716360793fcbba627e621382cd3b312022ff47db: scope-index inventory does not list genesis definition pins or enumerate all registered scopes per Worker. Its v1 manifest stores service/root/scopes[{at,genesis}], with genesis an entry hash, and traverses one Directory creation tree plus its Register, expressly excluding that Register's other rooms (packages/cli/src/scope-index-manifest.ts6–12,63–86,131). The existing scope-index versions --manifest FILE already rereads entry0 for every supplied row, checks full reference, applied genesis/address and saved hash, and emits rows[].definition plus explicit unavailable/mismatch/unsupported outcomes (159–196;scope-index-command.ts60–67). No addition is needed to list pins for those existing saved sets. A per-Worker consolidation could use those reports plus operator-owned service/namespace/root attribution; it must preserve gaps and cannot establish a Worker census. For all registered rows the missing capability is an authorized bounded enumeration input: ScopeIndexRows.list(after,limit\u003c=100) exists internally, but ScopeIndexPort and deployed ScopeIndex expose only has/register and there is no listing route (scope-index.ts10–38;worker.ts109–147). Smallest building blocks are that list, existing {at,genesis} rows and checked IndexReads.entry(scope,0); no new pin column or newest-pin substitution is needed. A child would need to choose its authorized input/transport, distinguish absent/unsupported index from empty, qualify concurrent pagination, and preserve failed reads. Unregistered legacy objects remain outside even a complete registered-row list. Register-mode backfill and enforce evidence remain separate. A Worker build UUID/catalogue omission is not a scope pin or absence proof. Source-only audit: no hosted query, deployment, tests, retirement or code change."}